'\" -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
.TH PT35-HELP 1 "2026-10-16" "PocketTerm35" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pt35-help \- show help for PocketTerm35 custom commands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pt35-help
.RI [ command ]
.br
.B pt35-help
.RI [ keyword ]
.br
.B pt35-help
.B --all
.SH DESCRIPTION
Scans
.I /usr/local/bin
for scripts carrying a
.B "# help: category | description"
metadata header and lists them grouped by category.
.PP
With no arguments all commands are listed. With an argument:
.IP \(bu 2
a known command name shows detailed usage (including the
.B usage
block extracted from the script itself);
.IP \(bu 2
anything else is treated as a keyword, matched against command names,
descriptions and categories.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --all
List everything, including scripts without metadata.
.TP
.B --raw
Plain text (no colour), for piping into a pager.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
pt35-help                # list all commands
pt35-help wifi           # detailed usage for wifi
pt35-help network        # search by keyword or category
pt35-help --raw | less   # browse in a pager
pt35 <TAB>               # bash completion
.fi
.SH NOTES
Command metadata lives in the scripts themselves, so the help text can never
drift out of sync with the code, and no separate command list has to be
maintained.
.PP
.B pt35-help
never executes the scripts it documents \(em it parses them. This is
deliberate: an earlier implementation ran
.I "<command> --help"
to gather extra output, which caused
.B pt35-help pt35-poweroff
to actually power the machine off.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR zhdesk (1),
.BR zhfont (1),
.BR zhime (1),
.BR wifi (1),
.BR temp (1),
.BR pt35-healthcheck (1)
